import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.Bdp1ZChm.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Argon2 adapter evidence","description":"","frontmatter":{"packageVersion":"0.17.0"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/ARGON2.md","filePath":"docs/ARGON2.md","lastUpdated":1792938867000}`),a={name:`docs/ARGON2.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="argon2-adapter-evidence" tabindex="-1">Argon2 adapter evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#argon2-adapter-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to “Argon2 adapter evidence”">​</a></h1><p><code>micro509/kdf</code> exposes raw byte derivation with an explicit backend boundary. Its bundled implementation is a native WebCrypto adapter for Argon2d and Argon2id version <code>0x13</code>. A portable implementation and a version <code>0x10</code> implementation are separate work. This is not a KDBX parser or an Argon2 PKCS#8/PFX encoding.</p><h2 id="governing-inputs-and-scope" tabindex="-1">Governing inputs and scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#governing-inputs-and-scope" aria-label="Permalink to “Governing inputs and scope”">​</a></h2><p>Research on 25 October 2026 used <code>bun spec list</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>census</code>, <code>headings</code>, and <code>read</code>. Census expressions were <code>Argon2</code>, <code>memory-hard</code>, and <code>password-based key derivation</code>. The expanded corpus contained 165 documents. RFC 9106 was fetched through <code>bun spec fetch rfc 9106</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Governing:</strong> RFC 9106 §3.1 (<code>docs/rfc/rfc9106.txt</code>, lines 189–226) defines parameter ranges and units. The adapter passes the specified memory value exactly. Rounding memory before passing it would change the initial hash. §4 lines 578–619 gives parameter-selection guidance. Salt minimum eight bytes is a native implementation boundary, not a universal RFC 9106 requirement.</li><li><strong>Governing:</strong> RFC 9106 §§5.1 and 5.3 (lines 625–689 and 753–803) supply the Argon2d/id vectors with secret and associated data. Both are exercised by the shared runtime smoke suite and the real optional backend in <code>test/kdf.test.ts</code>.</li><li><strong>Governing for the native adapter:</strong> Node&#39;s <a href="https://nodejs.org/api/webcrypto.html#class-argon2params" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Argon2Params documentation</a> specifies native field names and version <code>0x13</code>. Actual Node 26.12.0 and the project-installed Deno 2.9.6 produce the RFC vectors. A separate direct probe with system Deno 2.9.8 also matched. Bun 1.4.3 exposes Argon2 through its Node-compatible crypto module but not through WebCrypto.</li><li><strong>Format boundary:</strong> the <a href="https://keepass.info/help/kb/kdbx.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">KDBX format</a> permits <code>0x10</code> and <code>0x13</code>, records memory in bytes, and requires at least an eight-byte salt. Callers convert units and prepare the composite key.</li><li><strong>Analogous:</strong> RFC 9580 §3.7.1.4 describes the OpenPGP Argon2id profile. Its fixed profile values do not govern this raw KDF interface.</li><li><strong>Outside this change:</strong> RFC 8018 §6.2 specifies PBKDF2 for its PBES2 profile. RFC 9879 updates PKCS#12 password handling. These do not establish an Argon2 container encoding. PKITS and X.509 certificate path rules do not govern raw Argon2 derivation.</li></ul><p>The live RFC status observation reported no updates or obsoletions for RFC 9106. Verified editorial erratum 7721 concerns §3.2, outside this adapter&#39;s parameter-mapping implementation. RFC 8018&#39;s updates 9579 and 9879 were inspected for the container boundary. RFC 9580&#39;s reported updates and errata do not alter the raw RFC 9106 interface.</p><h2 id="independent-inputs" tabindex="-1">Independent inputs <a class="header-anchor" href="#independent-inputs" aria-label="Permalink to “Independent inputs”">​</a></h2><p><code>test/kdf.test.ts</code> uses a real <code>node:crypto.argon2</code> backend under Bun rather than replacing global crypto. It compares outputs with:</p><ul><li>RFC 9106&#39;s Argon2d/id vectors, including secret and associated data.</li><li>The Bitwarden SDK vector already exercised by the separate Vaultwarden client: UTF-8 <code>67t9b5g67$%Dh89n</code>, SHA-256 of <code>test_key</code> as salt, 32 MiB, four passes, two lanes, and 32 output bytes. The expected hex output is <code>cff0e1b1a213a34c626ab3afe00911f01493ed2ff6968db83ee183f23335e1f2</code>.</li><li>Independently generated raw KDBX-style key material using <code>@noble/hashes</code> 2.4.0: password bytes <code>0..31</code>, salt bytes <code>255..224</code>, 1 MiB, three passes, two lanes, and 32 output bytes, for both variants. These are raw KDF fixtures, not full KDBX files. <code>0x10</code> refusal is covered separately.</li></ul><p>The optional-backend contract is also exercised for snapshot ownership, temporary-array wiping, worker transfer of input buffers, genuine runtime failure, exact limit boundaries, malformed numeric values, and memory values that are not multiples of <code>4*p</code>.</p><h2 id="sample-performance" tabindex="-1">Sample performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#sample-performance" aria-label="Permalink to “Sample performance”">​</a></h2><p>Commands, each in a separate process after <code>bun run build</code>:</p><div class="language-sh" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/benchmark-argon2.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> native</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/benchmark-argon2.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> native</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 4</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/benchmark-argon2.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/benchmark-argon2.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 4</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Sample on Linux x86-64, Node 26.12.0, 25 October 2026. Each process performed three batches using 64 MiB, three passes, four lanes, and a 32-byte output. Other development work was active, so these are reproducibility examples, not a performance guarantee.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th style="text-align:right;">Concurrent calls</th><th style="text-align:right;">Median call (ms)</th><th style="text-align:right;">Process peak RSS (MiB)</th><th style="text-align:right;">Largest 5 ms timer delay (ms)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>micro509 native WebCrypto</td><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td style="text-align:right;">82.2</td><td style="text-align:right;">118.4</td><td style="text-align:right;">6.3</td></tr><tr><td>micro509 native WebCrypto</td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td><td style="text-align:right;">140.7</td><td style="text-align:right;">311.4</td><td style="text-align:right;">5.9</td></tr><tr><td>direct <code>node:crypto</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td style="text-align:right;">78.0</td><td style="text-align:right;">116.8</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.5</td></tr><tr><td>direct <code>node:crypto</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">4</td><td style="text-align:right;">148.0</td><td style="text-align:right;">308.4</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>RSS is the whole-process high-water mark reported by <code>resourceUsage</code>, including roughly 51–52 MiB baseline runtime memory. It is not an isolated allocation measurement. Both routes may use the same native crypto implementation, so this comparison measures adapter overhead and scheduling. It does not compare native Argon2 against a portable JavaScript/WASM implementation. The timer probe confirms that this sample allowed event-loop progress during native work.</p>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};